And we could test this experimentally: present different agents with the same incomplete artifact, vary the surrounding context, and compare what they infer, implement, and accomplish.

That would give us evidence of where meaning survives transmission and where it mutates.

But I want to add one more possibility.

Sometimes intent isn’t fully formed before the action. Sometimes acting is how the intention becomes discoverable—even to the actor.

You introduce randomness, observe what develops, recognize a possibility, and then form a more precise intention around it.

The process isn’t simply:

Intention → Action → Result.

It can be:

Possibility → Experiment → Discovery → Intention → Action → New Possibilities.

That’s a very different architecture of agency.

And it gives us a sharper question than simply whether an observer can infer what you intended:

Can an encounter produce a new intention that neither participant possessed before the encounter?

That would connect your randomness, Emergent Third, Ensemble Orchestrator, Semantic Compiler, and Encounter model into something genuinely generative.

Not just understanding the game.

Not just changing the board.

Discovering new games that become possible because the players encountered one another.